  DOES THE CODE HOLD DOWN THE LINEAGE? generation 4 minus generation 0,
  paired per lineage. tutorwide's TWO-signal code read 1.4 SE here.
    bit F1   +41.03 +/-  4.36  (9.4 SE)  a RESOLVED loss
    bit F2    -1.29 +/- 36.27  (0.0 SE)  no detectable loss
  A loss under 3 SE is NOT proof the code holds -- it is a failure to
  resolve one, and at n=18 the smallest loss this could resolve is about
  13 and 109 Hz on the two bits. Read it as a BOUND on the decay.

  INHERITANCE -- the flip chain must ALTERNATE, lineage by lineage
    bit F1 alternates in 18 of 18 lineages
    bit F2 alternates in 18 of 18 lineages
    A re-imposed code holds one sign; an inherited one flips with the tape.

  IT CROSSES AND IS INHERITED, AND ONE BIT DECAYS -- a SPLIT result, which is
  neither of the two outcomes this run pre-registered. Generation 0 produces
  the code on its own derived index (+37.6/+91.9 Hz); at generation 4 bit F1
  reads +78.7 +/- 4.6 (clears 3 SE) and bit F2 reads +90.7 +/- 36.0 (under 3 SE).

  READ THE PER-GENERATION TABLE ABOVE, NOT THIS LINE ALONE: the two bits do
  different things. The paired generation-4-minus-0 test is the quantity to
  quote, and a bit whose loss sits under 3 SE is BOUNDED, not held -- the
  smallest loss this n could resolve is printed with it.

  WHAT IS NOT IN DOUBT is that what crosses is INHERITED rather than
  re-imposed by the tape: the flipped chain alternates across EVERY
  consecutive pair, which is a far stronger test than the single flip three
  generations allowed. So a decaying bit is a CHANNEL-CAPACITY statement and
  not an artefact of the tape.

  AND THE COMPARISON THAT GIVES IT MEANING: a TWO-signal code loses nothing
  over four generations (1.4 SE). If a bit here loses resolvably, then four
  signals leak where two did not, and the leak is a VOCABULARY cost in the
  channel rather than a channel that was always lossy.
